{"z":"Hyndburn","x":"2018","y":71.4},{"z":"Hyndburn","x":"2019","y":79.3},{"z":"Hyndburn","x":"2020","y":79.5},{"z":"Hyndburn","x":"2021","y":75.1},{"z":"Hyndburn","x":"2022","y":69.5},{"z":"Hyndburn","x":"2023","y":72.1},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2012","y":63.6},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2013","y":64.8},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2014","y":61.4},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2015","y":67.6},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2016","y":74.3},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2017","y":73.6},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2018","y":66.8},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2019","y":69.1},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2020","y":74},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2021","y":75.4},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2022","y":67.4},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2023","y":68.4}],"selected":"Carlisle","padding-right":"90"},{"type":"Employment_level","content":"<p>Overall, there were 50,900 people aged 16 and over in Carlisle who were employed in the year ending March 2023. This is down from the previous year, when there were  52,500 people who were employed.</p>"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"type":"Unemployment","content":"<a name=\"section-unemployment\"></a><h2>Unemployment rate in Carlisle was 3.6% in the year ending March 2023</h2><p>Around 2,000 people in Carlisle were unemployed in the year ending March 2023. This is a rate of 3.6%.</p><p>Across the North West, from the year ending  March 2022 to the year ending March 2023, there was a decrease in the unemployment rate from 4.4% to 3.9%.</p><p>Year on year, the number of people unemployed in the North West fell from around 157,000 to around 138,000 over the same period.</p><p>Unemployment across Great Britain dropped between the year ending March 2022 and the year ending  March 2023, from around 1,350,000 people (3.6%) to around 1,200,000 (3.6%) in the year ending March 2022.</p><p>Unemployed people are those without jobs who are actively seeking work and available to take up a job. Some jobless people may not seek work or be able to work for various reasons, such as retirement, sickness, or study, and are called \"economically inactive\". </p><p>This means that the employment rate and the unemployment rate can both go up at the same time as people move into the labour market and become job seekers.</p><p>The unemployment rate is the number of unemployed people out of everyone  \"economically active\" in the labour market, that is, everyone who is employed or looking for work. The employment rate is out of the working-age population including  \"economically inactive\" people. </p>"},{"type":"ClaimantCount","content":"<a name=\"section-claimant\"></a><h2>Claimant Count in Carlisle was lower than a year earlier</h2><p>Claimant Count is an experimental statistic that measures the number of people who are <a href=\"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/aguidetolabourmarketstatistics#claimant-count\">claiming unemployment-related benefits.</a></p><p>The Claimant Count in Carlisle was 583.3 in the year ending July 2023, down from  the year ending July 2022, when there were 621.7 claimants. </p><p>The proportion of people in Carlisle aged 16 to 64 years in Carlisle who were claiming unemployment-related benefits was 2.6% in the year ending July 2023, decrease compared with the year ending July 2022 when it was 2.8%.</p>"},{"id":"chart-claimanttime","type":"Chart","chartType":"line-highlight","title":"Claimant count (%) in the North West, July 2012 to July 2023","footer":"Source: Claimant Count from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"z":"Manchester","x":"2013","y":5.2},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2014","y":3.5},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2015","y":2.7},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2016","y":2.7},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2017","y":2.5},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2018","y":3.2},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2019","y":4.2},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2020","y":8.7},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2021","y":8.1},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2022","y":6},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2023","y":6.2},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2013","y":2.9},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2014